33
00:01:46,950 --> 00:01:49,450
Okay, so first we're
going to be talking about

34
00:01:49,450 --> 00:01:52,790
MRSA. So just some basic
facts. It's gram-positive

35
00:01:52,790 --> 00:01:56,030
cocci found in clusters.
It's typically found in

36
00:01:56,030 --> 00:01:59,210
the nasal mucosa and the
skin, and when you see

37
00:01:59,210 --> 00:02:01,390
it clinically, it's going
to present as a nosocomial

38
00:02:01,390 --> 00:02:03,690
or opportunistic
infection, so they're going

39
00:02:03,690 --> 00:02:09,170
to result in pus-filled
wounds, abscesses, and

40
00:02:09,170 --> 00:02:12,170
they're just going to look
a lot like these images.

41
00:02:12,390 --> 00:02:15,690
And like the name says, these
are methicillin-resistant,

42
00:02:15,690 --> 00:02:17,870
so they're not going
to respond to oxacillin

43
00:02:17,870 --> 00:02:21,030
treatment, and they're resistant
to all other beta-lactams

44
00:02:21,030 --> 00:02:24,290
like cephalosporins,
carbapenems, so you need stronger

45
00:02:24,290 --> 00:02:27,950
agents like vancomycin or
daptomycin to treat MRSA.

46
00:02:29,330 --> 00:02:32,310
MRSA results from a
mutated MEK-A gene which

47
00:02:32,310 --> 00:02:36,090
produces a different
penicillin binding protein, and

48
00:02:36,090 --> 00:02:39,630
this protein has a low
affinity for beta-lactams,

49
00:02:39,630 --> 00:02:42,350
and that's why you can't
treat MRSA with beta

50
00:02:42,350 --> 00:02:46,270
-lactam antibiotics. And
because the mutated MEK-A

51
00:02:46,270 --> 00:02:49,870
gene is unique to MRSA,
that's what all these tests

52
00:02:49,870 --> 00:02:52,370
are looking for when
you're trying to detect it.

53
00:02:52,890 --> 00:02:56,770
And in addition to broth
micro dilution testing, the

54
00:02:56,770 --> 00:02:59,990
Clinical and Laboratory
Standards Institute recommends

55
00:02:59,990 --> 00:03:03,450
the cefoxitin disc
diffusion test, or a plate

56
00:03:03,450 --> 00:03:06,870
containing six micrograms
per mil of oxacillin in

57
00:03:06,870 --> 00:03:10,270
Mueller-Hinton-Auger be
supplemented with 4% sodium

58
00:03:10,270 --> 00:03:13,790
chloride as an alternate
method for detecting MRSA.

59
00:03:14,190 --> 00:03:16,990
And lastly, you could also PCR

60
00:03:16,990 --> 00:03:19,880
for the MEKA gene
to detect MRSA.

61
00:03:21,370 --> 00:03:24,750
Methicillin-resistant staph
aureus is highly resistant

62
00:03:24,750 --> 00:03:28,090
to drying, pH extremities,
and high temperatures

63
00:03:28,090 --> 00:03:32,210
and can result in biofilm
formation. It's becoming

64
00:03:32,210 --> 00:03:34,570
increasingly prevalent
in community-acquired

65
00:03:34,570 --> 00:03:38,070
cases with infections in
schools, prisons, and areas

66
00:03:38,070 --> 00:03:41,350
with shared equipment. So
all that being said, MRSA

67
00:03:41,350 --> 00:03:44,190
has become more prevalent
in society, and it's

68
00:03:44,190 --> 00:03:47,350
becoming more and more
difficult to treat with the

69
00:03:47,350 --> 00:03:52,770
rise of vancomycin-resistant
staph aureus. So you as

70
00:03:52,770 --> 00:03:57,170
a clinician want to make
sure that you are properly

71
00:03:57,170 --> 00:04:01,210
detecting, is this MRSA,
and what antibiotics is

72
00:04:01,210 --> 00:04:03,670
it susceptible to? So
you want to practice good

73
00:04:03,670 --> 00:04:06,570
antibiotic stewardship
so you don't harbor more

74
00:04:06,570 --> 00:04:10,210
antibiotic resistance and
ultimately create a superbug.

75
00:04:11,490 --> 00:04:13,730
The next pathogen we're going

76
00:04:13,730 --> 00:04:15,690
to be talking about is C. diff.

77
00:04:16,130 --> 00:04:19,350
C. diff is a bacterium
that causes diarrhea and

78
00:04:19,350 --> 00:04:21,970
colitis and the infection
can be life-threatening

79
00:04:21,970 --> 00:04:26,210
if not treated. And there
have been hypervirulent

80
00:04:26,210 --> 00:04:28,890
epidemic strains that
have emerged in the past.

81
00:04:28,970 --> 00:04:31,990
And when I was actually
on my third year inpatient

82
00:04:31,990 --> 00:04:35,210
IM rotation, there was
a small C. diff outbreak

83
00:04:35,210 --> 00:04:38,450
amongst some of the
residents and one or two of

84
00:04:38,450 --> 00:04:41,550
the attendings. So everyone
on the floor had to

85
00:04:41,550 --> 00:04:45,170
take special hand hygiene
and contact precautions.

86
00:04:45,350 --> 00:04:48,210
And again, this just
goes to show how

87
00:04:48,210 --> 00:04:51,490
relevant this lecture
is. So make sure to

88
00:04:51,490 --> 00:04:54,070
practice good hygiene
in the hospital.

89
00:04:55,210 --> 00:04:58,850
C. diff is an obligate
anaerobe and is a gram

90
00:04:58,850 --> 00:05:02,910
-positive spore-forming
rod with two toxins, A and

91
00:05:02,910 --> 00:05:06,510
B. Toxin A is an
enterotoxin. It binds to the

92
00:05:06,510 --> 00:05:09,630
brush border enzymes on
the enterocytes, and it

93
00:05:09,630 --> 00:05:12,710
creates inflammation and
electrolyte and fluid

94
00:05:12,710 --> 00:05:15,910
secretion, which is what
results in the diarrhea.

95
00:05:16,350 --> 00:05:20,450
And toxin B depolymerizes
the actin filaments inside

96
00:05:20,450 --> 00:05:24,130
the enterocytes, so that
leads to cell death and

97
00:05:24,130 --> 00:05:28,390
necrosis. And we typically
detect C. diff by testing

98
00:05:28,390 --> 00:05:33,030
the stools for the
enterotoxins because culture

99
00:05:33,030 --> 00:05:37,250
would take too long and the
toxin assay is immediate.

100
00:05:37,930 --> 00:05:41,650
As for treatment, first
line is oral fidoxamycin

101
00:05:41,650 --> 00:05:45,050
or oral vancomycin.
Second line is

102
00:05:45,110 --> 00:05:48,170
metronidazole and if
there are complications of

103
00:05:48,170 --> 00:05:51,870
C. diff like toxic
megacolon or necrotic colon,

104
00:05:51,870 --> 00:05:54,810
then you would need
surgical management.

105
00:05:55,410 --> 00:05:58,370
And if there are recurrent
cases of C. diff,

106
00:05:58,650 --> 00:06:03,130
then a fecal microbiota
transplant can be conducted.

107
00:06:04,710 --> 00:06:07,270
The next topic we're going
to be talking about is

108
00:06:07,270 --> 00:06:10,810
TB, which is caused by
mycobacterium tuberculosis,

109
00:06:10,810 --> 00:06:13,530
and it's an airborne
spread disease that's

110
00:06:13,530 --> 00:06:17,010
present in the lungs. So
TB is aerosolized, meaning

111
00:06:17,010 --> 00:06:19,550
that it just floats in
the air for several hours

112
00:06:19,550 --> 00:06:22,930
and it's highly contagious,
but active infection

113
00:06:22,930 --> 00:06:25,310
is typically present in
individuals with weakened

114
00:06:25,310 --> 00:06:27,850
immune systems. So these
are patients with HIV,

115
00:06:28,390 --> 00:06:29,050
AIDS,

116
00:06:29,210 --> 00:06:32,010
organ transplant
recipients, incarcerated

117
00:06:32,010 --> 00:06:34,530
persons, people living
in trailer homes,

118
00:06:34,710 --> 00:06:35,430
etc.

119
00:06:35,770 --> 00:06:38,630
And depending on how
immunocompromised an

120
00:06:38,630 --> 00:06:40,550
individual is, there's a
different classification

121
00:06:40,550 --> 00:06:43,690
on whether or not they
test positive on PPD skin

122
00:06:43,690 --> 00:06:46,190
testing, but that's
not really the focus on

123
00:06:46,190 --> 00:06:49,390
this lecture, we are
more focused on the micro

124
00:06:49,390 --> 00:06:52,470
-bio aspect of it, not
necessarily the clinical.

125
00:06:53,290 --> 00:06:55,890
Latent infection
of TB is treated

126
00:06:55,890 --> 00:06:57,890
with isoniazid for nine months.

127
00:06:58,070 --> 00:07:00,910
An active infection of
TB is treated by ripe

128
00:07:00,910 --> 00:07:04,850
therapy, which is rifampin,
isoniazid, pyrazinamide,

129
00:07:04,850 --> 00:07:07,030
and ethambudol, and
those are taken for,

130
00:07:07,030 --> 00:07:09,570
those four drugs are
taken for two months, and

131
00:07:09,570 --> 00:07:12,530
then it's followed by two
drugs for four months.

132
00:07:12,530 --> 00:07:15,170
So those two drugs are
isoniazid and rifampin.

133
00:07:16,190 --> 00:07:21,530
And TB has evolved and
mutated such that it's begun

134
00:07:21,530 --> 00:07:24,790
to mutate in the genes
that are the active sites

135
00:07:24,790 --> 00:07:28,210
for where these drugs
act on. So for instance,

136
00:07:28,450 --> 00:07:32,110
an RNA polymerase gene or
a catalyzed peroxidase gene

137
00:07:32,110 --> 00:07:35,490
has been mutated so that
the drug can't act on it.

138
00:07:35,630 --> 00:07:40,390
And multi-drug resistant
TB is defined as

139
00:07:41,940 --> 00:07:44,850
tuberculosis caused
by an organism that is

140
00:07:44,850 --> 00:07:48,310
resistant to at least
isoniazid and rifampin,

141
00:07:48,310 --> 00:07:51,750
and these are the two
most potent TB drugs.

142
00:07:52,790 --> 00:07:57,230
Extensively drug-resistant
TB strains are defined

143
00:07:57,230 --> 00:08:01,770
as a TB strain that is
resistant to isoniazid and

144
00:08:01,770 --> 00:08:05,810
rifampin, as well as any
fluoroquinolone and one second

145
00:08:05,810 --> 00:08:08,810
-line agent that's
typically used to treat TB.

146
00:08:08,890 --> 00:08:12,910
And because extensively drug
-resistant TB is resistant

147
00:08:12,910 --> 00:08:15,770
to most of the potent TB
drugs we have currently,

148
00:08:15,770 --> 00:08:18,310
the remaining treatment
options are less effective,

149
00:08:18,310 --> 00:08:20,690
have more side effects,
and are more expensive.

150
00:08:21,130 --> 00:08:24,390
And drug-resistant TB,
whether that be multi-drug

151
00:08:24,390 --> 00:08:27,150
resistant or extensively
drug-resistant strains,

152
00:08:27,150 --> 00:08:29,790
are more common in people
who do not take their TB

153
00:08:29,790 --> 00:08:32,870
medicine regularly, don't
take all of their TB

154
00:08:32,870 --> 00:08:37,030
medications as prescribed
by their doctor, people who

155
00:08:37,030 --> 00:08:39,950
develop TB disease again
after having taken TB

156
00:08:39,950 --> 00:08:42,670
medicine in the past, and
they typically come from areas

157
00:08:42,670 --> 00:08:45,630
of the world where drug
resistant TB is common.

158
00:08:46,090 --> 00:08:49,530
And so the patient
comes to you and says,

159
00:08:49,530 --> 00:08:52,510
if I have drug
susceptible TB, how can

160
00:08:52,510 --> 00:08:56,590
I prevent from gaining
drug resistant TB?

161
00:08:57,160 --> 00:08:59,830
The most important thing
for you to do as a clinician

162
00:08:59,830 --> 00:09:03,690
is to counsel your patient
and advise them to take all

163
00:09:03,690 --> 00:09:07,510
their TB medications
appropriately and exactly as

164
00:09:07,510 --> 00:09:10,430
prescribed. No doses should
be missed and treatment should

165
00:09:10,430 --> 00:09:14,410
not be stopped early, even
after their symptoms stop.

166
00:09:15,090 --> 00:09:18,510
And options for treatment
of multidrug-resistant

167
00:09:18,510 --> 00:09:21,940
TB or extensively drug
-resistant TB include an

168
00:09:21,940 --> 00:09:24,930
abbreviated regimen, which
is around 6 to 12 months or

169
00:09:24,930 --> 00:09:28,150
a longer individualized
regimen, and these are

170
00:09:28,150 --> 00:09:31,750
typically for 18 months.
And so these regimens are

171
00:09:31,750 --> 00:09:36,350
typically tailored to the
patient and determined by

172
00:09:36,350 --> 00:09:39,490
which antibiotics that
strain is susceptible to.

173
00:09:40,010 --> 00:09:42,790
Typically the most
common regimens are the B

174
00:09:42,790 --> 00:09:46,150
-PAL and the B-POM. So
the B-PAL is a six-month

175
00:09:46,150 --> 00:09:49,690
all-oral regimen which
consists of betaquiline,

176
00:09:50,550 --> 00:09:55,310
protominid, and linezolid.
And B-POM is a six-month

177
00:09:55,310 --> 00:09:59,870
all-oral regimen consisting
of BPAL plus moxifloxacin.

178
00:10:03,630 --> 00:10:07,730
Neisseria gonorrhea is a
gram-negative diplococci

179
00:10:07,730 --> 00:10:11,570
which is oxidase positive
and not encapsulated. It has

180
00:10:11,570 --> 00:10:14,730
features such as pili,
the opa protein, and lipo

181
00:10:14,730 --> 00:10:18,550
oligosaccharides which allow
for antigenic variation which

182
00:10:18,550 --> 00:10:21,750
allows the pathogen to
escape or avoid a host immune

183
00:10:21,750 --> 00:10:24,790
response making it harder
to detect and allowing

184
00:10:24,790 --> 00:10:27,930
for possible sites for
mutation and drug resistance.

185
00:10:28,950 --> 00:10:33,450
So Neisseria gonorrhea
contains beta-lactamases,

186
00:10:33,490 --> 00:10:36,990
which is encoded by a plasma
gene, and so that makes

187
00:10:36,990 --> 00:10:40,950
N-gonorrhea resistant to beta
-lactam antibiotics, which

188
00:10:40,950 --> 00:10:44,290
includes penicillins and
encephalosporins, and then

189
00:10:44,290 --> 00:10:48,010
it's also resistant to
tetracyclines and fluoroquinolones.

190
00:10:48,010 --> 00:10:51,630
So if a patient has
positive for gonorrhea, you

191
00:10:51,630 --> 00:10:56,590
treat them with empiric dual
therapy for chlamydia and

192
00:10:56,590 --> 00:11:00,030
gonorrhea. So you treat
them with IM ceftriaxone as

193
00:11:00,030 --> 00:11:03,570
well as oral azithromycin.
So the ceftriaxone treats the

194
00:11:03,570 --> 00:11:06,750
gonorrhea and the azithromycin
treats the chlamydia.

195
00:11:08,310 --> 00:11:10,110
Okay, next we're
going to be talking

196
00:11:10,110 --> 00:11:12,950
about carbapenem
resistant enterobacter.

197
00:11:12,950 --> 00:11:15,430
And carbapenems are
the strongest beta

198
00:11:15,430 --> 00:11:17,690
-lactams and are therefore
the last case for

199
00:11:17,690 --> 00:11:20,870
antibiotic used so we
can avoid antibiotic

200
00:11:20,870 --> 00:11:23,550
resistance and not
create a superbug.

201
00:11:23,850 --> 00:11:26,670
And carbapenems are a
combination of penicillin and

202
00:11:26,670 --> 00:11:29,350
a cephalosporin. They're
used to treat nosocomial

203
00:11:29,350 --> 00:11:32,950
infections caused most commonly
by Klebsiella, E. coli,

204
00:11:32,950 --> 00:11:36,470
and Pseudomonas. However,
strains have become resistant

205
00:11:36,470 --> 00:11:40,250
to carbapenems through
efflux pumps, and this is

206
00:11:40,250 --> 00:11:42,750
when the antibiotic just
gets pumped out of the cell,

207
00:11:42,870 --> 00:11:45,450
decreased permeability of
the outer membrane so the

208
00:11:45,450 --> 00:11:48,250
drug can't even enter the
cell and carbapenemases.

209
00:11:48,970 --> 00:11:52,770
And a carbapenemase is
an enzyme that's found in

210
00:11:52,770 --> 00:11:55,050
resistant strains that
essentially breaks down

211
00:11:55,050 --> 00:11:59,130
the carbapenem nucleus and
just denatures the drug.

212
00:11:59,830 --> 00:12:04,410
And these carbapenem
resistant strains are unique

213
00:12:04,410 --> 00:12:07,070
because you can't even pair
them with a beta lactam

214
00:12:07,070 --> 00:12:10,050
inhibitor since they don't
even respond to them.

215
00:12:12,790 --> 00:12:16,410
Because carbapenems are
the strongest beta-lactam

216
00:12:16,410 --> 00:12:19,990
antibiotic when you have
a carbapenem resistant

217
00:12:19,990 --> 00:12:24,410
strain it's very difficult
to treat and mortality is

218
00:12:24,410 --> 00:12:29,210
very high and in the wards
you're gonna have contact

219
00:12:29,210 --> 00:12:31,350
precautions you want
to alert the facility

220
00:12:31,350 --> 00:12:34,590
infection control and laboratory
staff and these patients

221
00:12:34,590 --> 00:12:39,530
are typically closed
off treatment is usually

222
00:12:39,530 --> 00:12:42,990
consists of a combination
therapy with two or more

223
00:12:42,990 --> 00:12:47,290
drugs including one
carbapenem and if that doesn't

224
00:12:47,290 --> 00:12:49,970
work then you can resort
to polymyxins which can

225
00:12:49,970 --> 00:12:53,320
result in severe renal
toxicity and phosphomycin.

226
00:12:55,190 --> 00:12:58,690
Bortotella pertussis is an
encapsulated gram-negative

227
00:12:58,690 --> 00:13:03,490
rod and it's associated
with quite a few toxins. So

228
00:13:03,490 --> 00:13:06,830
the first is the pertussis
toxin which ribosylates and

229
00:13:06,830 --> 00:13:09,850
inactivates the G-inhibitory
protein resulting in an

230
00:13:09,850 --> 00:13:14,710
increase in cyclic AMP and
a lymphocytosis and this

231
00:13:14,710 --> 00:13:18,330
finding is unique to pertussis
specifically because usually

232
00:13:18,330 --> 00:13:20,410
when you think of a
bacterial infection you think

233
00:13:20,410 --> 00:13:23,940
of leukocytosis whereas here
you have a lymphocytosis.

234
00:13:24,870 --> 00:13:28,330
Then there's the adenylate
cyclase toxin and this

235
00:13:28,330 --> 00:13:32,990
increases cyclic AMP
intracellularly which causes

236
00:13:32,990 --> 00:13:35,830
fluid to leave the
intracellular space and enter

237
00:13:35,830 --> 00:13:39,790
the extracellular space
resulting in edema and

238
00:13:39,790 --> 00:13:43,170
therefore preventing
phagocytosis of the bacteria.

239
00:13:43,390 --> 00:13:45,910
Then you have the
dermonecrotic toxin,

240
00:13:45,910 --> 00:13:48,170
and this results in
local tissue damage,

241
00:13:48,170 --> 00:13:52,130
and the tracheal toxin,
which damages ciliated

242
00:13:52,130 --> 00:13:54,500
cells in the
respiratory epithelium.

243
00:13:55,530 --> 00:13:58,410
Classically, there's
three stages of infection.

244
00:13:58,410 --> 00:14:00,430
The first is the catorol
stage, and this is the

245
00:14:00,430 --> 00:14:03,730
infectious stage. It
lasts one to two weeks,

246
00:14:03,730 --> 00:14:06,360
and it's characterized
by symptoms of a usual

247
00:14:06,360 --> 00:14:08,590
URI, so patients are going
to present with a low

248
00:14:08,590 --> 00:14:11,370
-grade fever, nasal
congestion, and rhinorrhea.

249
00:14:11,470 --> 00:14:16,070
Then you have the
paroxysmal stage, and this

250
00:14:16,070 --> 00:14:18,230
can last two to eight
weeks, and this is what's

251
00:14:18,230 --> 00:14:20,670
characterized by the
classic whooping cough.

252
00:14:21,650 --> 00:14:24,350
And then the last stage
is the convalescent

253
00:14:24,350 --> 00:14:26,830
stage, and that can last
up to weeks to months,

254
00:14:26,830 --> 00:14:29,320
and it's just characterized
by that subsiding

255
00:14:30,570 --> 00:14:33,670
cough. Immunity to
pertussis isn't lifelong,

256
00:14:33,670 --> 00:14:36,090
that's why you need to
get vaccinated many times.

257
00:14:36,610 --> 00:14:41,350
Your first exposure to
the pertussis Ig molecule

258
00:14:41,350 --> 00:14:44,210
is in utero when mom
is given the Tdap

259
00:14:44,210 --> 00:14:47,490
vaccine at 28 weeks
gestation and then you get it

260
00:14:47,490 --> 00:14:50,630
three times as a child
as DTap and then every

261
00:14:50,630 --> 00:14:54,310
10 years as an adult
with the Tdap vaccine.

262
00:14:56,590 --> 00:15:00,710
Pertussis is highly
contagious and it's endemic

263
00:15:00,710 --> 00:15:04,390
worldwide in most densely
populated areas. It's so

264
00:15:04,390 --> 00:15:07,490
contagious that it's
given to women at 28 weeks

265
00:15:07,490 --> 00:15:10,710
gestation, not necessarily
to protect mom, but to

266
00:15:10,710 --> 00:15:14,590
protect baby. So mom will
basically make the IgG

267
00:15:14,590 --> 00:15:18,070
antibodies and that can
pass through the placenta

268
00:15:18,070 --> 00:15:21,550
and enter baby's circulation
such that if anybody

269
00:15:21,550 --> 00:15:24,290
wants to hug or kiss baby
and could potentially

270
00:15:24,290 --> 00:15:27,910
be infected with pertussis,
baby is protected.

271
00:15:28,610 --> 00:15:33,710
by the late 1990s there was
a rise in pertussis cases

272
00:15:33,710 --> 00:15:37,410
due to the anti-vaxxer
movement and here you can see

273
00:15:37,410 --> 00:15:41,350
that there was a slight
uptick in 2010 to 2015 in the

274
00:15:41,350 --> 00:15:46,390
pertussis cases and we are
seeing infections in vaccinated

275
00:15:46,390 --> 00:15:50,920
children due to waning
immunity so that's why you

276
00:15:50,920 --> 00:15:55,010
get the tdap or the DTaP
vaccine many times and there

277
00:15:55,010 --> 00:15:58,190
is some antigenic change
that's been seen in pertussis

278
00:15:58,230 --> 00:16:01,710
but the antigenic
change is very low

279
00:16:01,710 --> 00:16:04,190
and it can be controlled
by the vaccine.

280
00:16:06,170 --> 00:16:09,330
Cholera is a curved gram
-negative rod which is

281
00:16:09,330 --> 00:16:12,790
oxidase positive so it
can grow on the TCBS auger

282
00:16:12,790 --> 00:16:16,670
as seen in this image.
It's found in marine water

283
00:16:16,670 --> 00:16:19,930
and it prefers an alkaline
environment and it's

284
00:16:19,930 --> 00:16:23,750
acid labile, meaning that
it dies in acidic pHs.

285
00:16:25,750 --> 00:16:29,090
Infection with cholera
results in that classic rice

286
00:16:29,090 --> 00:16:32,290
water stool and it's
transmitted the fecal oral

287
00:16:32,290 --> 00:16:35,130
route due to poor sanitation
that can get into food

288
00:16:35,130 --> 00:16:37,790
and it's important to note
that it's a non-invasive

289
00:16:37,790 --> 00:16:41,370
infection. So basically the
bacteria attaches to the

290
00:16:41,370 --> 00:16:44,950
intestinal mucosa via the
fimbriae and it releases

291
00:16:44,950 --> 00:16:48,010
the cholera toxin, which
stimulates the GS protein.

292
00:16:48,010 --> 00:16:51,710
thereby activating adenylate
cyclase resulting in

293
00:16:51,710 --> 00:16:55,370
cyclic AMP and the cyclic
AMP then results in an

294
00:16:55,370 --> 00:16:59,150
efflux of chloride ions
and water into the lumen of

295
00:16:59,150 --> 00:17:03,290
the gut which then results
in that watery diarrhea.

296
00:17:04,130 --> 00:17:07,410
Treatment of cholera is
just oral rehydration

297
00:17:07,410 --> 00:17:10,790
and electrolyte replacement
and the CDC does

298
00:17:10,790 --> 00:17:13,410
define cholera as a
reportable illness and

299
00:17:13,410 --> 00:17:15,680
there have been some
outbreaks. So in 2010.

300
00:17:16,010 --> 00:17:18,770
In the Haiti earthquake
there was a cholera

301
00:17:18,770 --> 00:17:22,950
outbreak and then the
outbreak in Yemen due to

302
00:17:22,950 --> 00:17:25,710
the civil war which resulted
in very poor sanitary

303
00:17:25,710 --> 00:17:28,550
conditions and thus
the cholera outbreak.

304
00:17:28,690 --> 00:17:32,830
And although cholera isn't
a terrible disease compared

305
00:17:32,830 --> 00:17:36,170
to the others in this
lecture, it is very treatable.

306
00:17:36,170 --> 00:17:39,650
But when there is an
outbreak, it overwhelms

307
00:17:39,650 --> 00:17:41,570
and stresses the
health care system.

308
00:17:43,050 --> 00:17:46,410
Next, we're moving to
viruses. So the first virus

309
00:17:46,410 --> 00:17:48,650
we're going to be talking
about is measles, which is

310
00:17:48,650 --> 00:17:51,890
also referred to as rubeola.
It is a negative single

311
00:17:51,890 --> 00:17:55,130
-stranded RNA virus. It's
part of the peromyxovirus

312
00:17:55,130 --> 00:17:58,550
family, and it's spread by
droplet transmission. It

313
00:17:58,550 --> 00:18:02,870
was the leading cause of
child death in 2008, and the

314
00:18:02,870 --> 00:18:05,830
measles initiative started
in 2001 due to emergence

315
00:18:05,830 --> 00:18:10,070
of the disease because of
the anti-vaxxer movement.

316
00:18:12,590 --> 00:18:14,630
Measles was declared eliminated

317
00:18:14,630 --> 00:18:17,100
in the U.S. in the year 2000.

318
00:18:17,170 --> 00:18:20,590
So the disease did exist,
but there were zero cases

319
00:18:20,590 --> 00:18:23,210
reported in that year.
But then as the number of

320
00:18:23,210 --> 00:18:26,290
unvaccinated children began to
increase, the number of cases

321
00:18:26,290 --> 00:18:29,550
then started to increase,
which leads us to the study

322
00:18:29,550 --> 00:18:31,890
by Andrew Wakefield and
his colleagues, which was

323
00:18:31,890 --> 00:18:35,930
published in The Lancet in
1998. and in the study they

324
00:18:35,930 --> 00:18:38,750
suggested a possible link
between the MMR vaccine

325
00:18:38,750 --> 00:18:42,090
and the development of
autism in children and it's

326
00:18:42,090 --> 00:18:44,990
important to note that the
claims made in the original

327
00:18:44,990 --> 00:18:48,590
study have been thoroughly
discredited through a lot of

328
00:18:48,590 --> 00:18:52,370
research and investigations
but the paper has had a

329
00:18:52,370 --> 00:18:55,850
lasting impact on public
perception of vaccines and has

330
00:18:55,850 --> 00:18:59,650
contributed to vaccine
hesitancy and the refusal in

331
00:18:59,650 --> 00:19:03,030
some communities. So you
as a clinician will have to

332
00:19:03,030 --> 00:19:05,850
deal with parents who do
have doubts on the vaccine

333
00:19:05,850 --> 00:19:09,450
and might even cite this
study, but it's important for

334
00:19:09,450 --> 00:19:15,050
you as the physician to
discredit whatever the study

335
00:19:15,050 --> 00:19:18,670
states. And it's important
to be empathetic, but it's

336
00:19:18,670 --> 00:19:24,030
also important to be factual,
and it's important to

337
00:19:24,030 --> 00:19:27,250
counsel parents, especially
on vaccine hesitancy and

338
00:19:27,250 --> 00:19:29,730
why it's important for their
child to get vaccinated.

339
00:19:32,230 --> 00:19:34,750
Measles is transmitted
through droplet

340
00:19:34,750 --> 00:19:36,810
spread and typically
presents with

341
00:19:36,810 --> 00:19:39,410
the four C's, which
is cough, choriza,

342
00:19:39,730 --> 00:19:42,610
which is just a runny
nose, conjunctivitis, and

343
00:19:42,610 --> 00:19:45,390
coplic spots, which are
these red spots with

344
00:19:45,390 --> 00:19:47,710
bluish-white centers seen
on the buccal mucosa,

345
00:19:47,710 --> 00:19:50,930
and these are pretty
pathognomonic for measles.

346
00:19:51,110 --> 00:19:54,490
It also presents as a rash
that starts on the face

347
00:19:54,490 --> 00:19:56,570
and then spreads downwards
throughout the whole

348
00:19:56,570 --> 00:20:01,130
body, and they're also
going to present with a

349
00:20:01,130 --> 00:20:04,190
sore throat and a high
fever. Signs and symptoms

350
00:20:04,190 --> 00:20:08,190
usually start 7 to 18 days
after initial infection.

351
00:20:09,730 --> 00:20:14,210
If you suspect that a patient
has measles, you could

352
00:20:14,210 --> 00:20:18,970
test their antibody titers
to see if they have some

353
00:20:18,970 --> 00:20:22,910
prior immunity to measles,
so you want to check their

354
00:20:22,910 --> 00:20:26,810
IgG and IgM titers specifically,
but as for the most

355
00:20:26,810 --> 00:20:29,910
part, measles is a
clinical diagnosis which is

356
00:20:29,910 --> 00:20:33,450
highlighted by the rash, the
high fever, and the four C's.

357
00:20:33,690 --> 00:20:35,810
You could also do
a viral culture

358
00:20:35,810 --> 00:20:38,390
or PCR, but those take time

359
00:20:38,530 --> 00:20:41,910
and the treatment for
measles is primarily

360
00:20:41,910 --> 00:20:45,170
symptomatic, so you want
to give them acetaminophen

361
00:20:45,170 --> 00:20:50,350
or ibuprofen or possibly
vitamin A, but there

362
00:20:50,350 --> 00:20:52,810
is no treatment to
decrease the viral load.

363
00:20:54,810 --> 00:20:58,470
Mumps is a negatively
-sensed single-stranded RNA

364
00:20:58,470 --> 00:21:01,790
virus, which is also part
of the paramyxovirus family,

365
00:21:01,790 --> 00:21:05,930
and signs and symptoms for
mumps are pretty unique.

366
00:21:06,490 --> 00:21:09,350
It replicates in the salivary
glands, so it's going

367
00:21:09,350 --> 00:21:12,790
to result in the swollen
parotid glands. It can also

368
00:21:12,790 --> 00:21:15,810
replicate in the testes,
resulting in orchitis,

369
00:21:15,810 --> 00:21:18,450
which can result in impaired
fertility and testicular

370
00:21:18,450 --> 00:21:21,730
atrophy, and late
complications could include

371
00:21:21,730 --> 00:21:26,430
meningitis. And typically,
besides these pretty unique

372
00:21:26,430 --> 00:21:29,330
signs, patients are also
going to be complaining of

373
00:21:29,330 --> 00:21:32,170
a fever, headache, muscle
aches, loss of appetite,

374
00:21:32,170 --> 00:21:32,950
and fatigue.

375
00:21:34,990 --> 00:21:38,290
Like measles, mopsis
transmitted through

376
00:21:38,290 --> 00:21:40,550
droplet spread and
through fomites,

377
00:21:40,890 --> 00:21:44,890
measles is highly contagious.
So patients can be

378
00:21:44,890 --> 00:21:47,430
contagious one to two
days before the swelling

379
00:21:47,430 --> 00:21:50,190
start, so before they even
know they are infected

380
00:21:50,190 --> 00:21:52,570
with mumps, and five
days after the swelling

381
00:21:52,570 --> 00:21:55,310
subsides. So you want to
counsel your patients to

382
00:21:55,310 --> 00:21:58,730
limit contact even when
there's no more swelling.

383
00:21:58,730 --> 00:22:02,990
As for diagnosis, you can
check antibody titers. IgM

384
00:22:02,990 --> 00:22:05,750
would indicate a new
infection. IgG would represent

385
00:22:05,750 --> 00:22:09,910
some prior immunity. You
could also do a viral culture

386
00:22:09,910 --> 00:22:13,950
or PCR, but again this is
mainly clinical diagnosis.

387
00:22:14,510 --> 00:22:19,570
And it's important to
note that measles, mumps,

388
00:22:19,570 --> 00:22:23,190
and rubella are reportable
illnesses to the CDC.

389
00:22:25,390 --> 00:22:29,270
Polio was scheduled by the
World Health Organization

390
00:22:29,270 --> 00:22:32,570
to be eradicated in 2000,
but then was postponed

391
00:22:32,570 --> 00:22:36,230
to 2010. And polio was
actually in the news pretty

392
00:22:36,230 --> 00:22:40,670
recently. In July of
2022, there was a case of

393
00:22:40,670 --> 00:22:43,870
paralytic polio that was
found in an unvaccinated

394
00:22:43,870 --> 00:22:48,070
resident in Rockland
County in New York. And in

395
00:22:48,070 --> 00:22:50,930
September of last year, the
New York governor declared

396
00:22:50,930 --> 00:22:53,330
a state of emergency as
there was evidence of

397
00:22:53,330 --> 00:22:56,430
circulating polio. It was
found in wastewater samples

398
00:22:56,430 --> 00:22:58,830
in Rockland, Orange, and
Sullivan counties, as well

399
00:22:58,830 --> 00:23:02,950
as New York City. But
then in December of 2022,

400
00:23:02,950 --> 00:23:04,710
the New York State
Department of Health

401
00:23:04,710 --> 00:23:06,930
announced that this
state of emergency was

402
00:23:06,930 --> 00:23:08,670
no longer needed
because there were fewer

403
00:23:08,670 --> 00:23:10,850
samples of the virus
that were detected

404
00:23:11,150 --> 00:23:14,190
than what they were
concerned about. And this

405
00:23:14,190 --> 00:23:17,530
outbreak was in the news
for a while, especially

406
00:23:17,530 --> 00:23:21,670
because the vaccination
rate was only 60%, so there

407
00:23:21,670 --> 00:23:25,290
was essentially very low
herd immunity. And this

408
00:23:25,290 --> 00:23:27,250
strain of polio that
they found is primarily a

409
00:23:27,250 --> 00:23:30,150
GI illness, which would
result in poliomyelitis.

410
00:23:32,310 --> 00:23:36,030
monkey pox was first
discovered in 1958 when two

411
00:23:36,030 --> 00:23:38,570
outbreaks of pox-like
disease occurred in colonies

412
00:23:38,570 --> 00:23:42,310
of monkeys kept for
research it is endemic to

413
00:23:42,310 --> 00:23:45,390
central and western africa
and it's transmitted via

414
00:23:45,390 --> 00:23:50,250
direct contact treatment
option includes tecovirumat

415
00:23:50,250 --> 00:23:54,310
which is an fda approved
treatment for smallpox

416
00:23:54,310 --> 00:23:58,130
and which is caused by the
variola virus in adults

417
00:23:58,130 --> 00:24:01,010
and children but the use
of tecovirumat for the

418
00:24:01,010 --> 00:24:03,850
treatment of other orthopox
virus infections, such

419
00:24:03,850 --> 00:24:06,930
as monkey pox, remains
unapproved at this time.

420
00:24:07,410 --> 00:24:10,350
Patients with monkey pox
benefit from supportive

421
00:24:10,350 --> 00:24:12,950
care mainly and pain
control. However,

422
00:24:12,950 --> 00:24:15,910
serious presentations
could be potentially

423
00:24:15,910 --> 00:24:18,870
treated with tecovirumat.
It's not FDA approved

424
00:24:18,870 --> 00:24:23,290
yet, but it has shown
some clinical efficacy.

425
00:24:23,730 --> 00:24:27,670
and there is the Giniose
vaccine, which is

426
00:24:27,670 --> 00:24:30,150
a live attenuated
vaccine that can be used

427
00:24:30,150 --> 00:24:34,210
to prevent monkeypox.
It is a series of two

428
00:24:34,210 --> 00:24:38,170
doses that is administered
28 days apart.

429
00:24:40,510 --> 00:24:43,530
Next, we're going to be
talking about dengue fever,

430
00:24:43,530 --> 00:24:46,650
which is caused by a
positively sensed single

431
00:24:46,650 --> 00:24:50,530
-stranded RNA virus, and it's
spread by the Aedes aegypti

432
00:24:50,530 --> 00:24:54,170
mosquito. It typically
incubates for four to seven

433
00:24:54,170 --> 00:24:57,790
days, and it infects the
bone marrow, and there's

434
00:24:57,790 --> 00:25:00,950
a spectrum of presentations
of dengue based on the

435
00:25:00,950 --> 00:25:04,590
viral load, so it can range
from very mild flu-like

436
00:25:04,590 --> 00:25:09,170
illness to dengue hemorrhagic
fever, which is very

437
00:25:09,170 --> 00:25:12,330
severe. And typically,
patients will present with

438
00:25:12,330 --> 00:25:15,930
pain in the back, joints,
muscle, bone, and eyeballs,

439
00:25:15,930 --> 00:25:19,070
and the severity of the
pain depends on what

440
00:25:19,070 --> 00:25:22,330
the viral load is. So if
they have a higher viral

441
00:25:22,330 --> 00:25:24,930
load, then they will have
a more severe presentation.

442
00:25:26,910 --> 00:25:30,710
So one of the most severe
presentations of dengue

443
00:25:30,710 --> 00:25:34,470
is dengue hemorrhagic
fever, which occurs in a

444
00:25:34,470 --> 00:25:38,410
few patients, but it is
very severe. The mortality

445
00:25:38,410 --> 00:25:42,670
is 15% with no treatment,
which is very high.

446
00:25:43,350 --> 00:25:47,390
and it results in renal
failure, septic shock,

447
00:25:47,390 --> 00:25:50,130
and potentially even
death if not treated.

448
00:25:50,130 --> 00:25:51,910
And even with treatment, it can

449
00:25:51,910 --> 00:25:53,910
result in these very
severe symptoms.

450
00:25:54,010 --> 00:25:56,030
And there's four
serotypes of the

451
00:25:56,030 --> 00:25:58,590
virus, and it's
diagnosed via PCR.

452
00:25:58,650 --> 00:26:02,510
And something that's unique
to dengue is that there's

453
00:26:02,510 --> 00:26:05,110
no cross-reactive immunity.
So what that means

454
00:26:05,110 --> 00:26:08,330
is a person can get infected
with the dengue, develop

455
00:26:08,330 --> 00:26:11,610
antibodies, and recover
from dengue, but then

456
00:26:11,610 --> 00:26:15,510
when they become reinfected
they will actually have a

457
00:26:15,510 --> 00:26:19,710
more severe presentation
and they will be weakened

458
00:26:19,710 --> 00:26:24,650
further. So reinfection
can be fatal with dengue.

459
00:26:25,050 --> 00:26:29,190
And traditionally it is
endemic in the tropics,

460
00:26:29,190 --> 00:26:33,730
particularly the Indian
subcontinent, the Philippines,

461
00:26:34,190 --> 00:26:40,230
Sub-Saharan Africa, but
But lately, there have been

462
00:26:40,230 --> 00:26:43,390
cases found in Key West,
Florida, and Texas, and it's

463
00:26:43,390 --> 00:26:47,330
actually been moving northward
due to climate change.

464
00:26:47,810 --> 00:26:51,230
And there is a vaccine
that has been developed,

465
00:26:51,230 --> 00:26:54,870
and although it decreases
the incidence of dengue

466
00:26:54,870 --> 00:26:59,650
fever, it contradictorily
increases the incidence

467
00:26:59,650 --> 00:27:03,090
of dengue hemorrhagic
fever. So even though

468
00:27:03,090 --> 00:27:05,310
you have an overall
decreased incidence of the

469
00:27:05,310 --> 00:27:09,030
disease, the mortality
of the disease has been

470
00:27:09,030 --> 00:27:14,750
higher. So that's been a
bit unusual, and that's why

471
00:27:14,750 --> 00:27:17,590
there hasn't really been
